    {
      "id": "c2-l3",
      "title": "Characters",
      "xp": 15,
      "intro": [
        "char holds a single character, written in single quotes: char grade = 'A'; Double quotes \"A\" would be a string, which is something else.",
        "Inside, a char is just a small number, its ASCII code. 'A' is 65, 'B' is 66, 'a' is 97. That's why you can do math with letters: 'A' + 2 is 'C'.",
        "%c prints a char as a letter, %d prints its number."
      ],
      "example": {
        "code": [
          "#include <stdio.h>",
          "",
          "int main() {",
          "    char c = 'A';",
          "    printf(\"%c is %d\\n\", c, c);",
          "    c = c + 2;",
          "    printf(\"then %c\\n\", c);",
          "    return 0;",
          "}"
        ],
        "output": [
          "A is 65",
          "then C"
        ]
      },
      "exercises": [
        {
